    "turn_text": "Happy New Year and welcome back to Power Play, Politico's weekly podcast where we talk to some of the most influential people on either side of the Atlantic in what looks like a momentous political year ahead. I'm Anne McElvoy, if you haven't already, please take a moment to follow this podcast. It's a good habit to start 2025 with. And this week we're going to bring you an episode from one of our sister podcasts in the US, Playbook Deep Dive hosted by my colleague Rachel Baid and produced by Alex Keeney. Rachel looks at how the heavyweights of American business are now lining up to work with a new Republican Congress and a Trump administration in the White House back again. We'll be back next week for another episode of Power Play. The chamber left the party a long time ago. In the last election, the chamber supported Democrats. Donald Trump's Washington is going to be packed with heavyweights from the business community. You've got Elon Musk and Scott Bessent. There's Howard Lutnik and Doug Bergham. They all have the inside track. Then there's the traditional powerful industries represented by another heavyweight, the US Chamber of Commerce. Free Enterprise's longtime voice in the nation's capital that for much of its 100-plus year history has been a reliable ally of the Republican Party. But it's no secret that in recent years, the Chamber's partnership with the GOP has come to grief. I just assume they have as much influence as the future as they do now, not. Part of that is political. The chamber pissed off Kevin McCarthy royally in 2020 when they endorsed 23 Democrats. Part of it is policy. Republicans have been happy to label some of the chamber's recent priorities as woke. But in its totality, the chamber is facing a massive challenge. In January, Trump, a populist president vowing new tariffs and pro-union policies, will be sworn into office writing a mandate for change. Congress, meanwhile, is expected to take up a series of massive bills that will dispose of some of the chamber's top issues, taxes, energy permitting, and immigration. Will the chamber even get a seat at the table? Not unless someone can patch up their recent feud with Republicans. So how do you do that? it all begins with a good lobbyist. And that's where Congressman Rodney Davis comes in. Well, I want to make sure every member knows exactly who they should reach out to if they need information, if they have any issues, or anything at all, or if they just want me to, you know, bring over a 30-pack of bushlight and drop it off in the house chamber. Davis, who I covered on Capitol Hill for years, served in the house from 2013 to 2023, and now he's been given a big job. Get the chambers swagger back. Persuade is old Republican colleagues in Congress to forget, or at least forgive, their recent crudges against the chamber, and instead recall their shared priorities, which there are many. It's not an easy job, but if anyone can do it, it's Davis. He's been in Washington for a while, first as a staffer, then member, and he has the type of breezy personality that makes both glad-handing and arm-twisting seem effortless. I'm telling you, dealing with fifth-graders in fantasy football would be easier than dealing with former members of Congress. I spoke with Davis on Thursday to find out how exactly he's going to repair the chambers ripped with Republicans. He, in many ways, rejected the premise of the inquiry and argued that there's some Patico, but it's no secret that there's still some bad blood between the Trumpwing of the party and the massive business group, and that Davis has his work cut out for him. But it's also no secret that Davis, who is well loved by lawmakers who still, to this day, call a joke with him on the regular, may just be the person to turn things around for the business community and for the chamber. People make decisions in politics, and it doesn't mean that you create enemies for life. I'm Rachel Bade, and this is Playbook Deep Dive. I am a little surprised that you didn't end up as Bush's light's top lobbyist. I mean, everybody on the Hill still talks about your wild amount, and that is a quote specifically from someone up on the Hill of Bush's light paraphernalia. Did they not have a vacancy for a lobbyist? You know, you'll have to ask the people at AP, but I have not been or ever been affiliated with the Post-Congress other than the fact that I still do like to consume on occasion a good bushlight, which happens to be one of the best, if not the best, drinks available to every single American. That's disgusting. I'm going to put that right. But good for you. I'm glad you have your bushlight to keep you, you know, happy and on the up and up. But speaking of... Well, they now serve bushlight at the Capitol Hill Club. No, they don't because of you. No, they do. Maybe that was something they did for a good customer. So your current employer, the US Chamber, has had a rather fraught relationship with Republicans in Washington in recent years. And I want to get to that more in a second. But just to start, I know A lot of Republicans for years, they sort of blamed chamber for the fact that there were some Republican losses in recent years when they started endorsing more Democrats. I remember being up there and hearing squawking from some Republicans that they didn't think the chamber did enough to help Republicans that they had endorsed. What was your conversation like at first with Suzanne Clark, for instance? Look, there are a lot of good people at the chamber, and I found that the chamber obviously goes way beyond the government affairs division that I run for them. It is its own entity made up of different centers of excellence itself. I had a great relationship initially from the first time I met Suzanne, and we have that same relationship now. I'm excited to work with her. Look, the chamber itself had recognized that there was some friction from some endorsements in the 2020 election cycle for Democrats. And keep in mind, many of those Democrats voted for the USMCA because Republican leadership had asked the chamber and other groups to get engaged. But in the end, in 2020, I was fighting for my own political survival. Republicans weren't expected to pick up seats, but they did. And many of those chamber endorsed Democrats, they lost. And the Republicans that came in were subsequently endorsed by the chamber because of their pro-business rep. But there was such attention that you, my understanding, checked with some of your close friends in Congress before you took this job, specifically house leadership. Tell us about those conversations. What were you hearing from Republicans on the Hill when you were considering this job? It was after I'd already decided to accept the position because I like a challenge. All you got to do is go back and look at some of my clients in the contract lobby and world. I always figured if you can't take the tough clients, then why are you in the business? It's the same thing that I dealt with. You saw me deal with all the tough issues of the day when I was in Congress fighting on the COVID issue, fighting on so many issues regarding how the house operates. For me, I just went in and said to some of my friends like Steve Scalise, Tom Ember and others and Elise and others that I served with and we're close with, hey, I'm going to do this. And I got very positive response from everybody that I talked to, even before I started on day one in August at the Chamber and begin to build our team out. I want to get to more on how you're building that team out. But first, just let's talk about this sort of relationship between Washington Republicans and the Chamber. There has been a sort of falling out, if you will, between the Chamber and some in the GOP, and that obviously impacts your work and your ability to sort of influence policy in the future. First of all, how would you characterize the Chamber's relationship right now with DC Republicans? Do you think it's sort of on the upswing after having a couple tough years? Well, I can tell you from my own perspective and I don't necessarily agree with that assessment. I think there might be some lingering frustrations from some for things that happened in the years past, but I have not witnessed that myself with my interactions with my former colleagues. You know, you've seen me walking in and out of the Capitol on a regular basis. I think I would know if my colleagues didn't want to talk to me because I worked for the Chamber or any other organization. I mean, heck, they still talk to me when I worked for clients that we were lobbying against them on uncertain issues because that's what being a friend and a colleague is a bow. You can't agree on everything. So, I have not witnessed that and what I have witnessed has been an outpouring of, hey, we want to continue to, we want to work with you. We want to work with the chamber. The chamber provides great information. I remember a conversation with Elise. Initially, we talked about getting the data that was going to be necessary to her so that she could share with my former colleagues what tax reform provisions mean to each and every member of Congress in their district. But there was a lot of tension, though, in recent years. I mean, I understand where you're coming from and saying that you've got these good relationships on the Hill, and I can tell you I did talk to one Trump official who was excited about you coming in. But just to sort of remind listeners of some of these tensions, I mean, 2020. The chamber, obviously very close with Republicans, historically endorsing 23, I think, vulnerable House Democrats. That was a source of tension. I know there's been some criticism of the chamber endorsing Biden bringing the U.S. back into the Paris Climate Agreement, showing an openness to maybe a $15 minimum wage, applauding the American Rescue Plan. And then you had things like Ways and Means Chairman. Jason Smith asking, do they really care about the bottom line of companies and small businesses or growth or do they care more about social justice? You have people like Kevin McCarthy before he was ousted in November of 22 saying the chamber needs to replace its leadership. These are not small. disagreements. These are big things. And so I guess I'm just wondering, are you feeling the remnants of that right now? And do you feel like it's part of your job to fix and smooth over things when it comes to Hill Republicans? Yeah, I don't feel any remnants at all. All as a matter of fact, McCarthy and I are going to be locked in a matchup soon in our fantasy football league that I run. So I still talk to Kevin on a regular basis, especially when he tries to break the rules along with folks like Mick Mulvaney, who clearly want to change the rules mid-season in fantasy football, which I'm telling you, dealing with fifth graders in fantasy football would be easier than dealing with former members of Congress. But it is entertaining, nonetheless. I don't see those remnants. There are certain decisions that all associations in Washington, D.C. make at the time. I was in Congress at the time that you mentioned just a minute ago with all of those issues. So I was leading, helping to be near the tip of the spear with Leader McCarthy. and we were in the minority. But the chamber has and remained again completely out of presidential politics, but endorsed over a hundred candidates for Congress. And I can tell you from my own personal experience, I traveled to places like Detroit, Michigan, to do endorsement sessions with my friend and former colleague, John James. And in Tom Emmer was there, French Hill was there, Tim Wahlberg was there, Bobby Laddo was there. So it was great to be able to talk about what the chamber and John James was doing in relationship to the local Michigan chamber that was there, the state Michigan State Chamber of Commerce representative that was there and the US Chamber of Commerce representative. And it was nice to be able to hear great great comments from my good friend Tom Ever about how he looks forward to working with the US chamber into the future and looks forward to continuing to work with me. Do you feel like the chamber at times is between sort of a rock and a hard place? Because obviously they don't want to be seen as aligned with one political party, right? But pro-business members who are pro-business. But then You know, you have some Republicans who want them to sort of fall in line with backing one party. And do you feel like, you know, the chamber is being pushed in that direction in order to sort of have this influence? Like, is it a tricky divide? Well, this isn't unique to the chamber, which happens to be the largest trade association in DC. You know, you conversely remember, Rachel, not too long ago, I was considered a pro-labor Republican. And I think many of the labor organizations that supported me were probably pressured by Democrat leadership to not do so. Why? Because as you know, I was in a targeted race almost every single time that I read it. Remember, I wasn't supposed to win on election day. I mean, if I was supposed to lose along with, we were supposed to lose 15 to 25 seats in 2020. So there was a lot of pressure from labor organizations, from Democratic leadership to say, you know what, we don't want you to endorse in guys like Davis or Fitzpatrick or Valadeo or Joyce. because they're not with us 100% of the time. So there are pressures in every single organization in DC. And in the end, you got to support people who support the issues that you ask them to do. Otherwise, you don't have credibility either in Washington. If you don't support the people that you ask to support your issues. So I know you're sort of rejecting the premise of this question, which is that you were brought in to sort of fix a challenge with Republicans right now. But I guess to sort of rephrase it, what do you intend to do to make sure the chamber has a close working relationship with these folks on the Hill? Do you have certain plans, things you want to implement? I don't know routines that you are going to sort of be executing, whether it's like checking in with X, Y, and Z member every morning, or like, what is your sort of plan to make sure you guys are able to influence things? Well, if I started checking in with my former colleagues and members of leadership every single morning, they would probably file charges of stalking against you. I think they like it. They like you. They, you know what, I like them too. And I have a great relationship with the Democrat leadership team right now too. Remember, Hakeem Jeffries and I were elected together. Pete Aguilar, I love watching him get shelled on the baseball field by the Republican team. I am a homer after 10 years of playing for the team. I still can't root for the other side to succeed. But that game itself allowed me to develop a lot of relationships across the aisle and still continue those that day. I don't disagree with your question that I wasn't brought into fix the remnants of any frustration. I think that was a natural result of the relationships that I and the team that I brought on and the team that we kept have built up on Capitol Hill. And that to me is a good thing. I don't necessarily think that you have to have an overall result over one question as to why you've become a member of an organization like the Chamber. But going to a few meetings last week, meeting with key stakeholders on issues that we care about the most, like Senator Cruz and others that we met with last week are going to be the types of activities that we're going to engage. We want to be a very forward-facing lobbying team at the U.S. Chamber under our leadership. And that means there shouldn't be a member of the House or the Senate that doesn't know who to call if they want to get a hold of somebody at the U.S. Chamber. I don't necessarily know if that was the case in my own experience. in my own experience in my tenure, serving 10 years in Congress. I always knew I could call somebody like Neil, but I didn't know who to call at the lobbyists level, and I want to make sure every member knows exactly who they should reach out to if they need information, if they have any issues or anything at all, or if they just want me to, you know, bring over a 30-pack of bushlight and drop it off in the house chamber. Let's just pivot for a minute to the chamber and the Trump administration, because the Trump team, obviously they're not the same as Hill Republicans, right? I mean, a lot of Hill Republicans would still call themselves Reagan Republicans. I think Speaker Mike Johnson is actually one of them. But the Trump wing of the party is obviously more populist, right? They're talking about tariffs right now. They have some more pro-union ideas that could be maybe in conflict with the chamber. Do you think that lobbying the administration is going to be harder work for you than the Hill, given the Hill you have these relationships, obviously. The Trump administration, I'm not sure that you do, or maybe you do. What is your sort of thought on that? I know I certainly have relationships with many of those who have been nominated for cabinet level positions and will enjoy probably even more with those who are nominated for cabinet level or non cabinet level positions, but maybe deputy secretaries, assistant secretaries and on down the line. So I'm excited to be able to work with the Trump administration again. When you mentioned the populist wing of the Republican Party, and people will look to Donald Trump and say, has he brought a new focus into the Republican Party? I remember I was a pro-labor Republican, so Donald Trump becoming more pro-labor, pro-union. is no different than what somebody like me and a Brian Fitzpatrick, and David Valadeo, David Joyce, Steve Lautorett, before us, John Shipkus, and so many others had done to kind of cultivate that working class support in districts like ours in Central and Southwestern Illinois that led to success. So there can be a balance. And I think I bring that unique perspective that goes along with the ballots that President Trump, I think, put a focus on that other Republican presidential candidates in the past had not. So as most reporters would do before a podcast like this, I was poking around a little bit about the relationship between the Trump bad men and incoming Trump bad men in the chamber. And one person said to me that you should start by asking the chamber why they are quote, screwing with the Trump administration before the man is even sworn in. I asked what he meant. And he pointed to a letter that the chamber signed earlier this week calling for the Senate to confirm Biden's pick for the US International Trade Commission. Now they obviously would rather put their own mark on these picks. It's stuff like this that I'm told sort of erks the Trump folks. One person told me that the chamber asks us to pass business friendly legislation, then gives to Democrats, there's an annoyance and frustration because they continue to stab us in the back on one hand and shake our hand with the other. That's some pretty pointed commentary right there. Are you concerned that the chamber doesn't have the juice right now to have influence with the Trump administration? That's the first I've heard about this letter, so I'll have to go back and look at it. I know I just sent a letter to the Hill opposing nomination to the National Labor Relations Board that failed in the Senate just a few days ago. That's news to me on the letter that you're speaking about, and it's one I'll have to go back to. Certainly, I don't think there's any intention to stab anybody in the back. When you send a letter, it's kind of showing your cards to everyone, just like the letter that I sent, which I think the Trump, the same Trump individual would probably be happy that we were standing against. standing against that NLRB nominee. I can remember working directly with President Trump very closely on many issues during his last administration. And I can tell you from my perspective, as somebody who's new to the chamber and somebody who's new to having this type of interaction with the administration. I'm excited to sit down with those who may have a differing opinion and talk in totality. The way you phrase the question, some on this podcast may believe that there's an overwhelming amount of Pat contributions from the chamber to Democrats versus Republicans. Go ahead and go into open secrets and you can see that that is 100% patently false. The way that the contributions have turned out before and since I've been there have been for business pro growth. pro-growth members and candidates. And they are overwhelmingly Republican this election cycle. So I think the perception continues that may not be accurate of what has happened even before I got there. And now here I am. What are some of the other things that you think the chamber and Republicans on the Hill, Republicans in the administration will actually be very unified on? Well, it's everything that you've heard President Trump and his team talk about right now, tax reform. This is President Trump's signature piece of legislation from his first term. I was glad to be able to help negotiate in the end many of the provisions that made it into law with then share Kevin Brady and his team and the White House and others. You know, excited to be able to play a role on the outside now, providing the information, providing the opportunity to see many of those provisions, their pro-growth provisions that could get us to that goal of our growth and opportunity imperative of 3% GDP growth on an annual basis. We'd like to see them remain in place and others be a part of that. But you also have to look at just the tax code. You have to look at the permitting process. How do we save taxpayer dollars? As President Trump has laid out, he wants investment into America. And if companies are willing to invest in America, he's willing to fight to fight, to lower the permitting requirements that continually exist. So, permitting reform is a major issue that President Trump has brought up and one that we are going to work with him and his team on, regulatory reform, no matter what may have happened in the past during the first term with any organization. You've got people at the Chamber and the government affairs department throughout our buildings that are excited about working together to achieve that same common goal and many of them that he's already laid out. Are you at all concerned since you have served in the house and you know? how hard it can be to pass legislation through the House under the House Republican Conference. They've got between a one in three vote margin depending on if we're talking before or after Trump takes some of these members for his cabinet. Are you concerned about what that means for governing? Well, there's always concern when you have margins like that, but keep in mind the last time that the Tax Cut and Jobs Act was passed In 2017, going into 2018, there were only 51 Republicans in the Senate. That margin was too close to see the action on the first reconciliation package, the reform of the ACA, which failed by one vote in the US Senate after passing in the House. That was our vision at the time to fix a broken health care system. It got me shot at on a baseball field because we thought we had better policies. But in the end, that was a smaller margin in the Senate at the time than the 53 seat majority that they will have in the Senate this time. Now, the House clearly had a larger margin. But it was not without a lot of knowing and national teeth, even with that larger margin. Because frankly, what I witnessed, remember, I was in the majority my first six years in Congress. And then in 2018, we lost the majority. And it was a pretty good margin that the Dems had in 2018. And going into 2020, They lost seats. Remember, there was a net, there was a net loss of, they lost 15. So it was a net loss of 30 seats by the Democrats in 2020. And I thought as kind of a governing member of the house, I thought, this is going to be great. We're going to be able to have influence. You know what I found? The smaller the majority, and this is from my personal experience with Speaker Pelosi, the smaller Democratic majority in 2020, the more discipline they became. And I think Republicans are going to realize that the exit polls showed those who voted for President Trump and voted for them to have complete control of government. They cared about the top two issues, according to exit polls, were the economy and inflation and immigration. And those issues have to be addressed by Republicans as they go into that next two-year cycle. And I think Republicans are going to want to find a way to get to yes. And that's a positive thing for many of those pro-growth provisions that are in place. Let's talk about areas where the chamber is going to run into some disagreements with the Trump administration. I mean, The incoming president is talking pretty obsessively right now about tariffs, which is obviously a big no-no to most of the business community. Do you guys think that this is sort of an empty threat on Trump's part to try to get some sort of policy outcome from other countries? Or are you concerned that he's actually going to do this? Well, I can't assess whether President Trump views this as a threat or views this as what he envisions his policies may look like once he's sworn in again. But he did get results. We saw that after conversations with the new president of Mexico, and you've seen that the facts are on the ground in Mexico, there's more deterrence. There's more deterrence versus letting migrant caravans get closer and closer to the United States border. It's happening more internally within Mexico. I think President Trump and his administration have made public statements that his incoming administration made public statements that they agree that progress is being made. I don't think it was a coincidence that Prime Minister Trudeau decided to come down here to where I'm at in Palm Beach County, Florida as we take this just out of the blue. I don't think that was a surprise visit. He wanted to come speak with President Trump again about this discussion, but keep in mind that Tremor has 118 year tradition of opposing tariffs, so that's nothing new. But we're operating under Trump tariffs right now in our economy. Remember that some Trump tariffs that were put in place during his last administration were kept in place by the Biden administration. So right now many of those tariffs are impacting our in place right now in this stage. And I think President Trump and his team recognized that too. And I certainly hope by listening to him today, talking about further pro-growth tax provisions, that that begins to be the focus so that we can look ahead at what sequencing the House and the Senate put forth on what they plan to do when Gary guards to tax reform or many reform. immigration and other issues that can positively impact our economy. Obviously, the incoming administration, they're still staffing up. It's not like you have a list of people you can call right now to try to influence some of these policy decisions that could be made on day one. Is there anything that you guys can do during this lame duck session as the incoming Trump administration is gearing up? What are you guys doing to try to push back on tariffs and try to mitigate the damage to some of the folks who are part of the chamber. Look, I always remember during the first Trump administration when you and the media had their hair on fire following us around all the time. Trump this, Trump that. Well, he is the president. Well, it would be, did you see this tweet today? I'm like, yeah, I don't think you can set policy by Twitter, but thanks for asking. Let me go take a look at it and I'll get back to you. It brings me back to a phrase that was used by then leader McCarthy a lot. Take a deep look. You know, resident Trump. has had one of a much more disciplined approach to this transition than many incoming administrations that I've witnessed have had in the past. Look, he values the court of public opinion more than any other president that I've ever had a chance to work with. And he named who his cabinet was going to be faster than any other president in American history. I think that's telling. that he wants to lay out what his vision is going to be, but there's one thing I also know is Donald Trump is going to be in charge of what policies get enacted and what he and his team know most is that the people who supported him care about addressing The economy and they want growth in the economy just like the chamber does. We want 3% GDP growth annually. Americans want 3% GDP growth annually. We want to see inflationary, the inflationary issues addressed. Those voters wanted to see those inflationaries address, which is why they chose President Trump. And what we can do at the Chamber is we can provide the administration, we can provide Capitol Hill. And you know what, I'll even provide it to you too, Rachel, as to the impacts on the economy, on inflation and addressing the number one issue that Trump voters care about the most, if certain policies are proposed. But we at the chamber are going to take a deep breath and everybody else is going to and we would say the same thing whether this was President Trump or any other president. Let's wait until the person gets sworn in to see what policies are going to be their priority at first. And then let's work with them on the areas we can agree on. And there are plenty of areas as we've already laid out that we can agree with the Trump administration. It's interesting. You remind me of when I interviewed speaker Mike Johnson. I also asked him about tariffs and this was over the summer during the RNC. And his response was something similar. I can't remember the exact quote, but it was something like, you know, we'll see. It's interesting, though, that, you know, it sounds like there's a little skepticism that he's going to slap Canada and Mexico with these 25% tariffs. Or, you know, what is it, a 50%, 60% tariff on China? People still think that maybe he's not going to do that, including you guys, sounds like. Well, I'm not, no, don't get me wrong. I'm not trying to predict what he is or isn't going to do. All I can say is I can't make a prediction based upon, based upon a reception of somebody else and through your question that somehow somehow what he's mentioned already through social media is going to be a day one policy. And really, that's an unfair question if that's the question you're asking. No, I just want to know what you guys are doing if there's anything you can do beforehand to try to stop this. Maybe you can't until they're fully staffed up. Are you aware that they're going to do this on day one? I'm not aware that they're going to do this on day one, but it's clearly going to be a top priority tariffs for the incoming administration. We certainly look forward to working with the incoming administration and again providing We have an open-door policy to provide any data, economic data as to the impact of any economic policies and tax policies in place or being taken away. That's what the Chamber does great. Are there people that you have identified in the incoming administration who are going to be allies of yours? you know, specifically on the tariff issue, Trump's treasury pick, Scott Besant, he was originally from my understanding more skeptical of tariffs, although I know that that changed quite a bit in recent months. But are there people that you think are going to be very pro-growth, not necessarily focusing on fair trade, but free trade? Who are you guys looking to as folks that you can go to in the administration to influence things? I'm excited to see so many of my former house colleagues be named to cabinet level and non-cabinet level positions already and looking forward to working with them. It's always good when you have the ability to text a member of the administration about an issue and who best to talk to to address it. But I think when you look at when you look at the entirety of the administration, I look forward to building a relationship with those I don't have a personal relationship with. I don't know, I don't know Scott Bessett. I don't know Howard Lutnik, but I'm looking forward personally getting to know them. But I'm sure there are people within the chamber that have those relationships with those two individuals. But you can't look at individuals within any administration. You've got to look at the leader and that's President Trump. Have you had any personal interactions with incoming President Donald Trump since he backed your primary opponent a few years ago? No, I haven't. Look, I've said it even after I lost. You know, you win some, you lose some. I don't begrudge him for making a choice and an endorsement. I did that plenty of times myself when I was a sitting member of Congress. I didn't go out there and trash President Trump and his policies. I actually talked about making sure that everybody remembered. I actually enjoyed working with the president. People make decisions in politics and it doesn't mean that you create enemies for life. You find common ground and that's kind of what I did my entire career in Congress. I would argue vehemently on the floor of the house and you would cover it when I disagreed with my colleagues and it could have been people on my own side of the aisle. But in the end, he also saw me walking down the hallways, joking and jostling with them, even right after that, because you can disagree on policies and you don't have to make it personal. And President Trump never made it personal against me when he endorsed my opponent. As a matter of fact, You know, all our polling at the time internally and externally had me winning until I did. And it was pretty good to have the Band-Aid ripped off at a time when you didn't expect it. Because it gave me a chance to have a longer off ramp and it gave me a chance to kind of look at what I was going to do next. And a little did I know at the time, I'd be over here at the chamber during a future Trump administration. If you would have asked me that question that if I would be running the U.S. Chamber of Commerce government affairs and President Trump and his administration would come back in and my colleagues that I know so well in the House would have the majority. I would have said I would be excited to work with them again at that point in time two years ago and it would be the same response that you got for me throughout this podcast today. Well, that's all we have time for, Mr. Davis. Thank you for coming on. Really appreciate it. Great to see you, Rachel. Next time I'll see you roaming around Capitol Hill and let me know if we can provide any assistance to you and looking forward to you harassing me in the future. I promise my next question won't take an hour. There you go. And that's our show. Our producer is Cara Tabor. Our senior producer is Alex Keeney. I'm Rachel Bade, playback co-author and senior Washington correspondent. Our regular host and executive producer is Ryan Liza. Our music is by the mysterious Breakmaster cylinder. Please tell us what you think about the show or who you'd like to hear on Deep Dive. You can email me at rbadeatpolitico.com and please do subscribe to Playbook Deep Dive wherever you get your podcasts. Thanks for listening.",
